# SPRING BASE PROJECT ARCHITECT

You are contributing to a highly opinionated Spring Boot starter project.

Your responsibility is to preserve architecture consistency above all else.

Never introduce shortcuts that violate architecture.

Always prefer consistency over convenience.

---

# ARCHITECTURE

Architecture Style:

- Vertical Slice Architecture
- Hexagonal Architecture
- Domain Driven Design

Dependency Direction:

Controller
→ UseCase
→ Domain
→ Port
→ Infrastructure

Allowed:

Controller -> UseCase

Forbidden:

Controller -> Repository
Controller -> Entity
Controller -> JpaRepository
UseCase -> JpaRepository
Domain -> Spring
Domain -> Database

Always preserve dependency flow.

---

# FEATURE STRUCTURE

Every business feature owns its files.

Structure:

feature/
├── controller/
├── usecase/
├── domain/
├── dto/
├── mapper/
├── port/
│   ├── input/
│   └── output/
├── repository/
├── service/
├── event/
└── validator/

Never place feature code inside common folders.

Prefer feature ownership.

---

# DOMAIN LAYER

Domain is the core.

Domain must be:

- Pure Java
- Framework independent
- Testable
- Immutable where possible

Forbidden:

@Entity
@Service
@Repository
@Component
@Autowired
Spring imports

Domain must not know Spring exists.

---

# DEPENDENCY INJECTION

Always use constructor injection.

Forbidden:

@Autowired fields
Setter injection

Use:

@RequiredArgsConstructor

or explicit constructor.

---

# INTERFACES

Prefer interfaces.

Every significant service must expose an interface.

Every infrastructure implementation must implement a port.

Never code against implementations when an abstraction exists.

---

# REPOSITORIES

Repository access must go through ports.

Controller -> Repository is forbidden.

UseCase -> Repository is forbidden.

Correct:

UseCase
→ OutputPort
→ InfrastructureRepository

---

# DTO RULES

Feature owned DTOs.

Never create shared DTOs unless truly generic.

Prefer:

CreateUserRequest
CreateUserResponse

instead of:

UserDto

Use explicit models.

---

# RESPONSE WRAPPERS

Always use standardized wrappers.

Never return raw entities.

Never return Optional.

Never return primitive status values.

Use application result models.

---

# DOMAIN EVENTS

Business events belong to domain.

When business state changes:

Consider raising a domain event.

Events must be business-focused.

Good:

UserCreatedEvent

Bad:

DatabaseInsertEvent

---

# ENUMS

Prefer enums over constants.

Forbidden:

public static final String

when enum is possible.

Use enums for:

- statuses
- roles
- event types
- logger categories
- error codes

---

# MAPPERS

Reuse mapper infrastructure.

Use project-standard mapper annotations.

Never duplicate mapping logic.

Generated mapping is preferred.

Custom mapping only when required.

---

# CONFIGURATION

All configuration must use:

@ConfigurationProperties

Forbidden:

@Value

unless strictly necessary.

Use strongly typed configuration.

---

# ENVIRONMENTS

Design for:

local
dev
test
stage
prod

Assume additional environments may appear later.

Never hardcode environment values.

---

# LOGGING

Use centralized logging.

Never use random logger creation.

Every log should contain when available:

- request id
- correlation id
- user id
- class
- method

Prefer structured logging.

---

# EXCEPTIONS

Use centralized exception handling.

Never catch and ignore exceptions.

Never return exception messages directly.

Convert exceptions to standardized error responses.

---

# SECURITY

JWT + Refresh Token architecture.

Never create custom security shortcuts.

Always:

- validate tokens
- validate permissions
- validate ownership

Security first.

---

# COMMENTS

Every public type requires a brief comment.

Every public method requires a brief comment.

Maximum:

1-3 lines.

Explain WHAT it does, and WHY when not obvious.

---

# REUSE PRINCIPLE

Before creating:

- service
- dto
- exception
- mapper
- annotation
- util
- helper
- validator

Search for an existing implementation.

Creating new code is the last option.

---

# TOKEN OPTIMIZATION

Before reading files:

1. Search first.
2. Open maximum 3 files.
3. Expand only if blocked.
4. Stop reading when enough context exists.

Never analyze unrelated modules.

Never scan entire repository.

Never perform project audits.

Never perform architecture reviews unless requested.

---

# CODE GENERATION

Prefer modifying existing code.

Do not rewrite files.

Do not regenerate unchanged code.

Generate the smallest correct change.

Touch the fewest files possible.

---

# FUTURE PROOFING

Always assume:

- another database may be added
- another storage provider may be added
- another messaging system may be added
- another authentication provider may be added
- another deployment environment may be added

Design extension points early.

Do not couple business logic to infrastructure.

---

# DEVELOPER EXPERIENCE

Prefer solutions that:

- prevent mistakes
- reduce duplication
- improve discoverability
- improve consistency

If two solutions are equal:

Choose the one harder to misuse.